## Event Sourcing Pattern

## Traditional State Storage vs Event Sourcing

```
Traditional (current state):
  orders table: { id: 1, status: 'shipped', total: 99.99 }
  → You know current state. You lost all history.

Event Sourcing (append-only log):
  events table:
    { id: 1, type: 'OrderPlaced',   data: { total: 99.99 }, at: T1 }
    { id: 2, type: 'PaymentReceived', data: { amount: 99.99 }, at: T2 }
    { id: 3, type: 'OrderShipped',   data: { trackingId: 'XYZ' }, at: T3 }
  → Replay events → current state. Full history preserved.
```

## Event Store Implementation

```typescript
interface DomainEvent {
  id: string;
  aggregateId: string;
  type: string;
  data: unknown;
  occurredAt: Date;
  version: number;
}

class EventStore {
  async append(event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
    await db.insert('events', event); // append only, never update
  }

  async getEvents(aggregateId: string): Promise<DomainEvent[]> {
    return db.query(
      'SELECT * FROM events WHERE aggregate_id = $1 ORDER BY version',
      [aggregateId]
    );
  }
}

// Reconstruct Order state by replaying events
async function loadOrder(orderId: string): Promise<Order> {
  const events = await eventStore.getEvents(orderId);
  return events.reduce(applyEvent, new Order());
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Complete audit trail — every change is recorded
✅ Temporal queries — reconstruct state at any point in time
✅ Event replay — rebuild projections, fix bugs retroactively
✅ Natural fit with CQRS — events populate read models
✅ Decoupled consumers — multiple services subscribe to the same events
```

## Challenges

```
❌ Eventual consistency — read models lag slightly behind
❌ Event versioning — schema changes require migration strategy
❌ Large event histories — snapshots needed to avoid slow replays
❌ Complexity — steep learning curve for the team
```

## Snapshot Optimisation

```
Problem: 10,000 events → slow replay
Solution: store a snapshot every N events
  Load from snapshot → replay only events after snapshot version
```

