## CQRS Pattern

## Origin

Bertrand Meyer's CQS: a method either **changes state** (command) or **returns a value** (query) — never both.

Greg Young extended this to the architecture level: separate the write and read **models**.

## Architecture

```
                    ┌──────────────┐
  Client ──────────▶│  Commands    │
  (mutations)       │  (Write Side)│
                    │              │
                    │  Aggregates  │
                    │  Domain Logic│
                    │  Repository  │
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │ emits events
                    ┌──────▼───────┐
                    │  Event Bus   │
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │
                    ┌──────▼───────┐
                    │  Projections │ ← event handlers update read models
                    │  (Read Side) │
                    │              │
                    │  Read DB     │ ← optimised for reads (Redis, PG views)
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │
  Client ◀────────────────┘
  (queries)
```

## Implementation

```typescript
// COMMAND: mutation
interface PlaceOrderCommand {
  customerId: string;
  items: { productId: string; qty: number }[];
}

async function placeOrder(cmd: PlaceOrderCommand): Promise<void> {
  const order = Order.create(cmd.customerId, cmd.items);
  await orderRepo.save(order);
  await events.publish(new OrderPlaced(order));
  // Returns void — commands don't return data
}

// QUERY: read model
interface OrderSummaryQuery { customerId: string; }

async function getOrderSummaries(q: OrderSummaryQuery): Promise<OrderSummary[]> {
  // Read from denormalised projection — simple, fast
  return redis.get(`orders:${q.customerId}`);
}

// PROJECTION: event handler updates the read model
async function onOrderPlaced(event: OrderPlaced): Promise<void> {
  const summary = buildSummary(event);
  await redis.set(`orders:${event.customerId}`, summary);
}
```

